//! lotus/crush.rye — ALES81: the bit-crusher — drop the low bits, and the map is no longer odd. The hard clip (ALES78), the overdrive (ALES79),
//! and the wavefolder (ALES80) all shape AMPLITUDE while holding the sample's RESOLUTION: every one of the 65,536 i16 codes still crosses. The
//! bit-crusher shapes the other axis — it drops the low bits, so a signal that entered at sixteen bits leaves at eight, or four, or one: the coarse,
//! gritty, quantized voice of an early sampler, a lo-fi drum machine, a digital signal starved of resolution.
//!
//! The crux is that DROPPING THE LOW BITS FLOORS TOWARD NEGATIVE INFINITY, so the bit-crush is the DRIVE family's FIRST map that is NOT odd. A crush
//! to `bits` bits keeps the top `bits` bits and clears the rest; clearing the low s = 16 − bits bits of a two's-complement integer is exactly
//! flooring to the grid of step 2^s, floor toward −∞ — the meaning of an arithmetic right-shift on a signed value: step = 1 << (16 − bits), q =
//! divFloor(x, step) · step. Every prior DRIVE map is odd (it shapes the magnitude and carries the sign); this one floors the SIGNED value, so
//! crush(−100) = −256 while crush(100) = 0 at bits = 8. That faint downward DC offset IS bit reduction's honest fingerprint — two's-complement
//! truncation floors, it does not round toward zero — and the rung states it positively rather than dressing the map in a symmetry it lacks.
//!
//! The grid of step 2^s (s ≤ 15) includes sample_min = −2^15 (a multiple of every such step) and every reachable grid point sits at or below x, so
//! q lands in [sample_min, sample_max] BEFORE the write: the shape does the bounding, and the saturate that follows is a documented no-op. The map is
//! IDEMPOTENT — a grid point crushes to itself, so crush(crush(x)) = crush(x). It is MEMORYLESS — every output depends only on its own input, so the
//! in-place read-then-write touches nothing another step needs, and crushing a span block by block equals crushing it whole. bits = 16 is the
//! IDENTITY (a shift of zero clears no bits), so the map generalizes the untouched clip.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES81.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-211634_fill-ales81-lotus-bit-crush.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/max, the one true saturate. It carries NO pre-gain and NO ceiling — the purest DRIVE
//! member; a keeper who wants a boosted crush runs lotus/drive.rye (ALES78) then crush over the same span, both in-place span maps that compose.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the resolution is a bit count, the grid a power-of-two step in sample units, and the shape a
//! quantizer, instantaneous (no attack/release, no dither, no anti-aliasing — a bit-crush is a heavy harmonic generator, and its quantization noise
//! folds in the i16 domain exactly as any integer decimator's). One divide, one multiply per sample; the crush only lowers resolution, so the output
//! fits the i16 rail by construction.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/crush.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");

pub const crush_version = "20260814.211634";

/// The widest resolution the i16 rail holds. A sample is sixteen bits, so a crush names between one bit (a two-level output) and sixteen (the
/// identity). Past sixteen there are no low bits to drop; below one there is no signal — both refuse BadBits.
pub const max_bits: u32 = 16;

/// The bit-crusher's faults, one consistent name each. BadRange coincides by name with the sibling drives and ALES2's timeline (Zig merges error
/// sets by name), so a composer sees one consistent span fault. BadBits is the bit-crush's own — the resolution axis no other DRIVE member names.
pub const CrushError = error{
    /// A resolution of zero (names no signal) or past sixteen (no low bits to drop) — refused, no write.
    BadBits,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Crush `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip` to `bits` bits of resolution: each sample's low `16 − bits` bits are cleared —
/// q = divFloor(x, step) · step with step = 2^(16 − bits), a floor toward −∞ onto the grid of that step. After the call every written sample lands
/// on a multiple of the step, its value is at most the input's (the floor never raises a sample), and every output depends only on its own input
/// (the crush is memoryless). `bits = 16` is the identity (step 1). Refuses BadBits on a resolution outside [1, 16] and BadRange on a span outside
/// the samples — each BEFORE any write, so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only — never the length.
pub fn crush(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, bits: u32) CrushError!void {
    // invariant: the resolution is legal — at least one bit (a two-level signal) and no more than the i16 rail's sixteen. Checked BEFORE any write.
    if (bits < 1) return error.BadBits;
    if (bits > max_bits) return error.BadBits;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder), exactly as the sibling drives.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const shift: u6 = @as(u6, @intCast(max_bits - bits)); // 0..15, so the shift is a legal amount for an i64 and 1 << shift never overflows i64
    // invariant: the grid step is a positive power of two no wider than 2^15, so sample_min = −2^15 is one of its multiples and the floor below
    // never drops a legal sample past the rail.
    const step: i64 = @as(i64, 1) << shift;
    assert(step >= 1 and step <= 32768);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const dry: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]);
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and dry <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // Clear the low bits: floor the SIGNED value onto the grid of `step`. @divFloor rounds toward −∞, the exact meaning of dropping the low
        // bits of a two's-complement integer — this is why the map is not odd (a negative sample floors DOWN, away from zero).
        const q: i64 = @divFloor(dry, step) * step;
        // invariant: the floor never raises a sample and never drops one below sample_min — the grid includes sample_min (a multiple of every
        // step ≤ 2^15) and the largest reachable grid point sits at or below the input, so q lands in [sample_min, sample_max] BY CONSTRUCTION and
        // the saturate that follows is a documented no-op.
        assert(q <= dry and q >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and q <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // invariant: the output sits on the grid — its low `shift` bits are clear (idempotence rests on this: a grid point crushes to itself).
        assert(@mod(q, step) == 0);
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(q);
    }
    // invariant: a crush changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: bits = 16 is the IDENTITY (a shift of zero clears no bits); DROP-THE-LOW-BITS by hand (bits 8, step 256, positives and negatives);
//     the ONE-BIT FLOOR (bits 1: non-negatives → 0, negatives → sample_min); every output is a MULTIPLE OF THE STEP and the map is IDEMPOTENT; the
//     RAIL is preserved (sample_min fixed, sample_max floors to 32512, every output in range under any bits); the map is NOT ODD, the honest DC
//     signature (crush(−100,8) = −256 ≠ −crush(100,8) = 0); the SPAN discipline holds; and each fault — BadBits, BadRange — refuses by name, the
//     clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. bits = 16 IS THE IDENTITY — a shift of zero clears no bits, so the crush returns the clip byte-for-byte. The map generalizes the untouched
    //    clip, the way ALES8's unity fader reproduces the plain render.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1, -1, 12345, -12345, 32767, timeline.sample_min, 0, 255 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 16);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.SixteenNotIdentity;
    }

    // 2. DROP-THE-LOW-BITS BY HAND — bits = 8, step 256, the floor read directly. 0→0; 255→0 (below the first grid step); 256→256 (a grid point);
    //    500→256 (floored down); −1→−256 (the floor goes DOWN); −256→−256 (a grid point); −257→−512 (the next step down). The negatives floor away
    //    from zero — the crux's asymmetry.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 0, 255, 256, 500, -1, -256, -257 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 8);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 256, 256, -256, -256, -512 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.LowBitsWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE ONE-BIT FLOOR — bits = 1, step 32768. Every non-negative sample floors to 0 and every negative to sample_min: a real two-level output
    //    {0, −32768}, the coarsest crush the rail holds.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 0, 1, 32767, -1, timeline.sample_min, 12345, -12345 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min, 0, timeline.sample_min };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.OneBitWrong;
    }

    // 4. EVERY OUTPUT IS A MULTIPLE OF THE STEP, AND THE MAP IS IDEMPOTENT — at bits = 4 (step 4096) each crushed sample lands on the grid, and
    //    crushing the already-crushed clip a second time changes nothing (a grid point crushes to itself).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 100, -100, 20000, -20000, 4096, -4096 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 4);
        const once = clip.samples();
        const step: i16 = 4096;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < once.len) : (i += 1) if (@mod(once[i], step) != 0) return error.NotOnGrid;
        var again = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&again, once);
        try crush(&again, 0, again.len, 4);
        if (!eq(again.samples(), once)) return error.NotIdempotent;
    }

    // 5. THE RAIL IS PRESERVED — sample_min is a grid point at every resolution (it floors to itself); sample_max floors to 32512 at bits = 8; and
    //    under any legal bits every output stays within [sample_min, sample_max], the floor never overflowing the rail it started inside.
    {
        const rails = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, 32767 };
        var b: u32 = 1;
        while (b <= max_bits) : (b += 1) {
            var clip = timeline.Clip{};
            try load_clip(&clip, &rails);
            try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, b);
            const out = clip.samples();
            if (out[0] != timeline.sample_min) return error.MinNotFixed; // sample_min is a multiple of every step ≤ 2^15
            if (out[1] > 32767 or out[0] < timeline.sample_min) return error.RailCrossed;
        }
        // sample_max at bits = 8 lands on the grid just below it.
        var eight = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&eight, &[_]i16{32767});
        try crush(&eight, 0, eight.len, 8);
        if (eight.samples()[0] != 32512) return error.MaxFloorWrong; // 32767 floored to a multiple of 256 is 32512
    }

    // 6. THE MAP IS NOT ODD — the honest DC signature. crush(−100, 8) floors DOWN to −256, while crush(100, 8) floors to 0, so crush(−x) ≠ −crush(x):
    //    the low-bit drop's downward floor, the fingerprint of two's-complement bit reduction that no odd waveshaper makes.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -100 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 8);
        const out = clip.samples();
        if (out[0] != 0 or out[1] != -256) return error.DcSignatureWrong;
        if (out[1] == -out[0]) return error.UnexpectedlyOdd; // −256 ≠ −0: the map is genuinely not odd
    }

    // 7. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — crushing a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the whole
    //    suite keeps. Crush the middle two of six at bits = 8; the first and last two are untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 500, -500, 300, 400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try crush(&clip, 2, 2, 8); // indices 2,3 only: 500→256, −500→−512
        const want = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 256, -512, 300, 400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 8. A ZERO / OVER-WIDE RESOLUTION refuses BadBits; a SPAN past the end refuses BadRange — each before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, 10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroBitsSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadBits) return error.WrongZeroBits;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroBitsTouched;
        if (crush(&clip, 0, clip.len, max_bits + 1)) |_| return error.WideBitsSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadBits) return error.WrongWideBits;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedWideBitsTouched;
        if (crush(&clip, 1, 3, 8)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-crush: the BIT-CRUSHER — drop the low bits, and the map is no longer ODD. The hard clip, overdrive, and wavefolder shape " ++
        "AMPLITUDE while holding RESOLUTION; the bit-crush shapes the other axis — it clears the low 16−bits bits, so a signal entered at sixteen " ++
        "bits leaves at eight, four, or one: the coarse quantized voice of an early sampler. Clearing the low bits of a two's-complement integer " ++
        "is flooring toward −∞ onto the grid of step 2^(16−bits) — q = divFloor(x, step)·step — the FIRST DRIVE map that is NOT odd: crush(−100,8) " ++
        "= −256 while crush(100,8) = 0, a faint downward DC offset that is bit reduction's honest fingerprint. bits = 16 is the identity (a shift " ++
        "of zero); drop-the-low-bits by hand at bits 8 step 256 (255→0, 256→256, 500→256, −1→−256, −257→−512); the one-bit floor yields a two-level " ++
        "output of 0 and −32768; every output lands on a multiple of the step and the map is IDEMPOTENT (a grid point crushes to itself); the rail " ++
        "is " ++
        "preserved (sample_min fixed, sample_max floors to 32512 at bits 8, every output in range under any bits); the map is not odd, the DC " ++
        "signature stated positively; the span discipline holds; a zero or over-wide resolution refuses BadBits, a span past the end BadRange, each " ++
        "leaving the clip untouched. The grid lands every output in [sample_min, sample_max] BEFORE the write, so the shape does the bounding and " ++
        "the saturate is a no-op; one divide, one multiply per sample; MEMORYLESS; no pre-gain, no delay line, no dither, no anti-aliasing, no " ++
        "socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. The DRIVE family now holds four shapes — the wall, the " ++
        "shoulder, the mirror, and the coarse grid; an asymmetric tube and a sample-rate decimator are the next\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
